<section class="reference-section" id="shiva-andhakasura"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Shiva and Andhakasura</h2><a href="/shiva-andhakasura.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Shiva Purana · Rudra Samhita · Yuddha Khanda · chapters 42 and 44–49.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the J. L. Shastri translation in the owner-approved source library. Extended speeches, graphic battle details and the intervening Shukra episode are condensed.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226183.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 42 · Andhaka’s birth and adoption</span><small>Verses 13–49: darkness at Mandara, Parvati’s care and Hiranyaksha’s adopted son.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226185.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 44 · Penance, sight and the demand</span><small>Mockery, boon, conquest, pride and the message sent to Shiva.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226186.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 45 · Parvati defends the threshold</span><small>Viraka’s guard, Parvati’s agency and the divine feminine allies.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226187.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 46 · Shiva returns</span><small>The continuing conflict and Andhaka’s subdual, retold without graphic detail.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226188.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapters 47–48 · The wider battle</span><small>The Shukra episode belongs to the source sequence but is outside this reader’s focus.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc226190.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 49 · Recognition and acceptance</span><small>Meditation, forgiveness, Parvati recognised as mother and a place among the Ganas.</small></a></div><p><strong>Reading care.</strong> Andhaka is born physically blind and later receives sight. The text then separately describes pride, desire and bad counsel as delusion. This page does not turn disability into a moral metaphor.</p><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> “Transformation” summarizes Andhaka’s own requests to relinquish enmity, recognise Parvati as mother and remain devoted among Shiva’s attendants. The reflection on accountability is editorial. Traditional narrative time is retained where the text speaks of three thousand years.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. The conflict remains non-graphic; the final image centres joined palms, a lowered crown and acceptance. No image is reused elsewhere.</p></div></section>
